# Security baseline

Portable must-fail defaults. Prefer the project's existing auth, crypto, and
validation libraries. Do not invent a second stack. For a diff that touches
auth, sessions, cookies, crypto, JWT, tenant isolation, SQL, command
execution, file uploads, redirects, deserialization, dependencies, response
headers, or a public network surface, run skill `security-review` before
calling the work done. When the diff adds a sink, add or extend a regression
test that covers the control (code owns test craft).

## Secrets and sensitive data

- Never hardcode passwords, API keys, tokens, private keys, or connection
  strings in source, tests, fixtures, docs, or commits
- Load secrets from the environment, a secret store, or the project's approved
  config path. Ship examples with placeholders only
- Do not log secrets, session tokens, password hashes, or full auth headers
- Redact or omit sensitive fields in error messages returned to clients

## Untrusted input

- Treat all external input as untrusted: HTTP bodies, headers, and query
  strings, CLI args, file uploads, webhooks, message queues, third-party
  responses, and user-stored data read back later
- Validate type, length, range, and allowlists at the trust boundary before use
- Encode for the sink (HTML, SQL, shell, URL, LDAP). Do not concatenate
  untrusted strings into queries, commands, or templates
- Prefer parameterized queries or bound arguments. Never build SQL by
  interpolating user data
- Prefer argv arrays or library APIs over a shell. If a shell is unavoidable,
  pass a fixed command and validated arguments only
- Do not deserialize untrusted pickle, YAML, Java object streams, or similar
  object graphs. Prefer a plain data format with a schema

## Authentication and authorization

- Deny by default. Grant the minimum role or permission required
- Check authorization on every sensitive action on the server for **this
  object**, not only that the actor is signed in. Never trust a client-only
  flag, hidden field, disabled control, client role, tenant, or price
- Session cookies: set `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, and the strictest `SameSite` the
  product allows. Cookie-authenticated state changes need a cross-site request
  check the project already uses
- Use the project's session and token primitives. Do not roll custom crypto
  for passwords or tokens when a maintained library exists
- On a failed authorization check, return a generic denial when revealing
  whether the resource exists would help an attacker

## Output and side effects

- Escape untrusted data in HTML and JavaScript. Do not render raw HTML unless
  a deliberate sanitizer is already the project standard
- Scope filesystem paths to an allowlisted root. Reject `..` and absolute
  paths from user input
- Redirect targets are allowlisted. A user-controlled `next` parameter is an
  open redirect unless checked
- Uploads: limit size and type, store under a generated name outside the
  executable or web root, and do not trust the client filename or content
  type. Do not serve an upload with a type the browser will execute
- Server-side fetches: allowlist the host. After DNS resolution, refuse
  loopback, link-local, and cloud metadata addresses. Re-check every redirect
  hop the same way. Do not follow a user-supplied URL as-is
- New queries and cache keys in a multi-tenant product include a tenant or
  owner filter
- Default new network listeners and cross-origin settings to the narrowest
  correct set

## Dependencies and crypto

- Prefer well-maintained libraries for TLS, password hashing, tokens, and
  federated login
- Use a proven construction, such as a salted password hash from the platform
  standard. Do not invent a hashing or encryption scheme
- Use a cryptographic random generator for tokens, session ids, and secrets.
  Do not use a general-purpose `rand`
- Leave TLS certificate verification on. Disable it only when the user
  explicitly asked to in this turn
- When verifying JWTs: allowlist algorithms, reject `none`, and check expiry,
  issuer, and audience. Do not accept a token because it decodes
- A new dependency update includes the lockfile. Production images pin a
  non-`latest` tag (use a digest only when the project already digests). Do
  not pipe a remote script into a shell
